<!--
# Hello Mail

A self-hosted mail server for two domains: one EC2 box running Stalwart, its
metadata in embedded SQLite on a persistent EBS volume and its message bodies in
S3, delivering its own outbound mail straight to each recipient's MX.

The point of the example is the pair of `<MailDomainBlock/>`s. A mail domain is
declared once, as the domain it serves, and everything composes from it: its
signing key, the `MX`/`SPF`/`DMARC`/`MTA-STS` records that make it deliverable,
the `SRV` and autoconfig records that make it discoverable, and the mailboxes it
holds. Serving a second domain is a second tag, which is exactly what the second
one here is.

The two are deliberately unalike, because that is what a second domain is for:
`example.com` holds people's mailboxes, and `news.example.com` sends
publications and welcomes replies into a catch-all. They share the box and the
zone and nothing else, so a newsletter that burns its reputation cannot take
anyone's mail down with it.

## No relay, and what that means

Neither domain declares one, so the box dials each recipient's MX itself. That is
the default and it is a real choice rather than a missing step: your address's
reputation is yours to build and yours to keep, nobody else can revoke your
sending, and there is no third party in the path of your mail. What it costs is
that a fresh address starts with no reputation at all, so early mail to the big
receivers lands in spam until a few weeks of ordinary correspondence fixes it.

Everything that makes direct delivery respectable is already here: an elastic
address whose reverse record `<EipReverseDns/>` sets to match the SMTP banner,
MTA-STS, TLS-RPT, DMARC at `p=reject` from the first deploy, and a DKIM key this
stack holds rather than rents. The apex SPF is `v=spf1 mx -all`, which authorises
exactly the box the `MX` already points at.

A relay is one spread away when you want one:

    <MailDomainBlock domain="example.com" .. {SesRelay}/>
    <MailDomainBlock domain="example.com" .. {ComailRelay}/>

See `mail_ses.bsx` and `mail_comail.bsx` for what each adds. Put the spread on
the `<Stack>` instead and every domain under it inherits, which is what a stack
that relays everything wants.

## Usage

    beet --main=examples/infra/mail.bsx validate
    beet --main=examples/infra/mail.bsx plan
    beet --main=examples/infra/mail.bsx deploy
    beet --main=examples/infra/mail.bsx destroy --force

`validate` needs only `tofu`. Live deploy needs AWS credentials, a Cloudflare
api token in `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN`, and `aws`/`ssh`/`curl` on the deploy
machine. It also needs domains you actually own: every name below is an RFC 2606
example domain and the zone id is a placeholder, so a real deploy replaces both.

Direct delivery also needs port 25 EGRESS from the box, which it has (the
security group allows all outbound) and which no relay arm needs at all. Note
that `<MailHealth/>`'s banner check dials port 25 INBOUND from wherever you run
it, and most residential ISPs block that, so run it from somewhere with 25 egress
or trust the probe instead.

Note the zone is written out on all three blocks (`dns={Cloudflare{..}}`) only
so the example validates with nothing configured, which is also why it repeats.
A real stack omits `dns` entirely and sets `CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID`: which zone a
launch writes into is a property of the launch rather than of any declaration,
so each block reads it from the environment and none of them restate it.

The box needs a zone of its own because the record it publishes is its own: an
`A` at `mail.example.com`, which every `MX` here targets and which its
certificate and reverse record are issued against. The box is infrastructure,
not a mail domain, so it owns that name rather than borrowing one.

## The deploy order, and why it is that

`<EnsureSecret/>` mints the bootstrap admin credential before the box boots to
read it. `<EnsureDkimKey/>` sits ahead of the apply for a sharper reason and is
not optional: the selector record reads its public half out of a tofu variable,
and a variable nobody supplied resolves to empty, which for `p=` is the wire
form of a REVOKED selector rather than a missing one. Neither value appears
here or in any log.

`<StalwartSnapshot/>` is the last thing before the apply, because the apply is
what may replace the box: it finds the data volume by its stack tags, waits for
a complete EBS snapshot, and only then lets terraform touch anything. A first
deploy has no volume yet and is skipped, a retry inside one deploy reuses the
deploy id as EC2's idempotency token rather than taking a second snapshot, and
the lineage it builds is pruned to its own newest `snapshot_retain`.

Then the reverse record (AWS validates the forward one first, so it waits for
dns), then `<StalwartProvision/>`. That last one is not a nicety: Stalwart 0.16
keeps its listeners, its routing, its domains and its accounts as objects INSIDE
its own data store, so a freshly built box serves nothing until they are applied
over its management api. Terraform owns the machine; provision owns the mail
server. Finally `<MailProbe/>` sends a message out through the whole stack and
reads one back in, asserting the receiving end believes both.

With no relay the probe's two legs are worth different amounts, and it says so
in its logs: the outbound leg is a real send to a third-party sink over a real MX
lookup, which is exactly what direct delivery has to prove, while the inbound leg
is submitted through the box's own port and so only ever exercises the local
route.
-->
<CliServer {CallOnReady}>
<Router {(RequestLogger, HelpHandler)}>
	<CrateCheck features={["infra", "extra"]}/>
	<!-- the region is a decision here (an mta near its users and its persistent
	     volume), so the stack pins it rather than inheriting `AWS_REGION`: a deploy
	     from a differently-configured machine builds the same stack. -->
	<Stack app_name="mail-example" region="us-west-2">
		<DeployRoutes/>

		<!-- the network is DECLARED, not assumed: this stack is one public box and
		     one EBS volume in the same zone, so it says so and emits one subnet.
		     The default (two zones, both tiers, four subnets) is what an
		     `<RdsPostgresBlock/>` subnet group requires. -->
		<VpcBlock bx:ref="net" label="net" zones={["a"]} private_tier=false/>
		<!-- message bodies: the source of record, so versioned and not
		     force-destroyable. -->
		<S3BucketBlock label="mail-blobs" deploy_versioned=false force_destroy=false
			runtime_write=true object_versioning=true/>
		<!-- the `archive` profile: cold, append-only, written by the box rather
		     than by any deploy. The nightly verified SQLite snapshot under `sqlite/`
		     is a copy and expires after a season; prefixes holding primary data beside
		     it would expire never. -->
		<S3BucketBlock label="archive" deploy_versioned=false runtime_write=true
			object_versioning=true
			expire_prefixes={[{prefix:"sqlite/", expire_days:180}]}/>

		<!-- the box, which is infrastructure rather than a mail domain: its name,
		     its reverse record, its certificate and its SMTP banner stay put while
		     the domains it serves come and go. Machine config remains replaceable;
		     its protected data volume survives and is reattached to the next box. -->
		<StalwartBlock label="mail" hostname="mail.example.com"
			blob_bucket="mail-blobs" backup_bucket="archive"
			{VpcRef($net)}
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"mail.example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			ssh_public_key="ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexam you@example.com"/>

		<!-- the domain people have mailboxes on. `postmaster@` and `abuse@` are the
		     two addresses an outside party actually tries when something is wrong,
		     so a served domain answers them from day one. -->
		<MailDomainBlock
			domain="example.com"
			mail_host="mail.example.com"
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			mailboxes={[
				{localpart:"hello", admin:true},
				{localpart:"probe"},
			]}
			aliases={[
				{localpart:"postmaster", target:"hello"},
				{localpart:"abuse", target:"hello"},
			]}/>

		<!-- the publication domain: its own identity and its own reputation, with
		     replies welcomed into a catch-all (a newsletter that bounces its
		     replies reads as unattended). Its reports go to the domain whose
		     mailboxes are read by a human. -->
		<MailDomainBlock
			domain="news.example.com"
			mail_host="mail.example.com"
			report_domain="example.com"
			dns={Cloudflare{authority:"news.example.com", zone_id:"0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef", proxied:false}}
			catch_all="replies"
			mailboxes={[{localpart:"replies"}]}/>

		<Route path="deploy" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<EnsureSecret secret="mail-admin-password"/>
			<!-- the sovereign signing key, minted before the apply that
			     publishes its public half: the selector record reads a tofu
			     variable, and a variable nobody supplied resolves to empty,
			     which for `p=` is the wire form of a REVOKED selector. -->
			<EnsureDkimKey/>
			<StalwartSnapshot/>
			<TofuApply/>
			<EipReverseDns/>
			<StalwartProvision/>
			<!-- with no relay the inbound leg is submitted through the box's own
			     port as the sending domain, so `sender` must name a mailbox that
			     exists on it rather than the default `news`. -->
			<MailProbe mailbox="probe" sender_domain="news.example.com" sender="replies"/>
		</Route>
	</Stack>
</Router>
</CliServer>
